{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>This is none other than the house of Elohim.</b> Rashi explained: Rabbi Eliezer said in the name of Rabbi Yossi ben Zimra — the ladder was standing in Be'er Sheva, etc. For when he departed from Be'er Sheva, which is in the land of Israel, angels of the land of Israel descended and accompanied him to the edge of the border of the land of Israel, and from there they ascended. And there, angels of outside the land descended to accompany him outside the land. Therefore one must say that the ladder was turning in every direction. The result was a ladder whose feet were in Be'er Sheva and whose head reached Beit El, which is Luz. Its middle slope was opposite Jerusalem. And there in Jerusalem stands a pillar on which the ladder turns. And מוצב refers to the pillar on which the ladder turns — its translation is נעוץ בארעא, fixed in the ground. And this is its form and structure.\\n\\n<b>This is none other than the house of Elohim.</b> Rashi explained: and from where did our Rabbis learn to say thus? That is to say: after our Rabbis interpreted on this verse 'a righteous man came to my inn,' which implies he lodged in Jerusalem — then they went back and interpreted that Jacob called it Beit El, and this is Luz, not Jerusalem. If so, these statements contradict those. Therefore I say that Mount Moriah was uprooted and came here to Beit El, and this is the contraction of the earth, etc. So Rashi explained.\\n\\nAnd the fact that Jacob went out of his way: for anyone who wishes to travel a straight route from Be'er Sheva to Haran goes from west to east, for Haran is to the east of the land of Israel, as it is written 'Aram from the east and Philistines from behind,' and Haran and Aram are one, as we said above. But he went from Be'er Sheva to Haran by way of Mount Moriah to Beit El, which is from south to north as far as Beit El, according to Rashi's interpretation.\\n\\nRather, one may answer: it is the road on which his grandfather Abraham came when he left Ur Kasdim and went to Haran, and from Haran to Be'er Sheva, and through several places — for on that road Abraham and Isaac settled, as we find in the preceding sections, and they were accustomed to that road and knew it. Therefore Jacob traveled on it.\\n\\nAnd what Rashi explained — 'perhaps I passed by the place where my fathers prayed,' etc. — he did not say this about Mount Moriah, for on Mount Moriah he prayed many times, and as our Rabbis said: no person can estimate how many libations Jacob our father poured on Mount Moriah. Rather, he said this about Beit El, for Abraham prayed there and built an altar on it, as it is written in the section of Lech Lecha: 'And he pitched his tent Beit El-ward, and Hai on the east, and he built there an altar.' Our Rabbis said: had Abraham our father not prayed between Beit El and Hai, no remnant or survivor would have remained of the enemies of Israel.\\n\\nAnd the fact that the place was called Beit El — it was called so for the future, for Luz was the name of the city at first. And Isaac likewise prayed at that same altar that Abraham our father built there, and even though we have no text and proof for this, we may say that Isaac certainly prayed at that same altar for many days and years. But Jacob had never been there and had not prayed there beforehand. And concerning this he said: perhaps I passed by the place where my fathers passed and did not pray there. He set his mind to return from Haran — where he was standing to go to Beit El to pray there. Immediately the earth contracted for him, i.e., the city that was already called Luz, and it came toward him as far as Haran. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: the prayer of a righteous man is heard only in a synagogue or in a study hall. Immediately Mount Moriah too was uprooted and came as far as Haran. After he prayed and sought to go, the Holy One, blessed be He, said: this righteous man has come to my inn and should depart without lodging? Immediately the sun set for him, as it is written: 'For the sun came, and he lay down in that place,' and he dreamed, and behold a ladder מוצב ארצה, etc." "<b>This is none other than the house of Elohim.</b> The explanation: this place I recognize, for I prayed in it many times, and I know that Mount Moriah came here. And since the place of the Temple came here, I call the city Beit El, by virtue of the Temple that came into it, as it is written: 'And he called that place Beit El, but Luz was the name of the city.'\\n\\nAnd Mount Moriah returned to its place, but the stone that he placed at his head, which was from Mount Moriah, remained there in Luz as a sign and testimony that Mount Moriah contracted there. And Luz too returned to its place, and Jacob and the stone with it, as it is written: 'And he took the stone,' etc., and from there Jacob arose in the morning from his lying down to go to Haran. And one cannot say that in Haran Jacob arose from his lying down, for it is written immediately after: 'And he went to the land of the children of the east,' and after it: 'And Jacob said to them, My brothers, where are you from? And they said, From Haran we are.' And upon his return from Bet Lavan he built an altar on it, as it is written in the section of Vayishlaḥ: 'And Jacob came to Luz, which is Beit El, and he built there an altar, and he called the place… And Jacob set up a pillar, a pillar of stone, and he poured a libation on it… and he called the place Beit El.'" "<b>And this is the gate of heaven.</b> Rashi explained: the Temple above is aligned opposite the Temple below — since it contracted toward Jacob, as he said above — therefore one must say that the Temple above also contracted toward him."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {2 "The Hebrew reads 'קפץ נגד יעקב' (contracted toward Jacob), which Rashi cites from earlier in the commentary; rendered consistently with the established translation of קפיצת הארץ as 'contraction of the earth.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:06:46.989143435Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}